tinker_cookbook.distillation.train_on_policy:201 [INFO] <|im_system|>system<|im_middle|>You are Kimi, an AI assistant created by Moonshot AI.<|im_end|><|im_user|>user<|im_middle|>How should I open a cover letter?<|im_end|><|im_assistant|>assistant<|im_middle|><think></think>Here are several effective ways to open a cover letter, depending on your situation:

## Strong, Specific Openings

**Name drop (if genuine)**
> "When Maria Fernandez from your engineering team mentioned you're looking for a senior developer, I knew I had to apply."

**Lead with a relevant accomplishment**
> "Last year, I grew my team's email subscribers by 340% using strategies I believe could directly support your Q3 goals."

**Show company knowledge**
> "I was impressed when [Company] announced its shift to renewable suppliers last month—it's exactly the kind of operational integrity I'd like to contribute to."

## When You Don't Have a Referral

**Connect mission to your motivation**
> "Your work on accessible financial tools aligns with why I became a designer in the first place."

**Address a specific problem they face**
> "Scaling customer support without losing personalization is a challenge I've solved twice before."

## What to Avoid

- "To Whom It May Concern" (find a name, or use "Dear Hiring Manager")
- "I am writing to apply for..." (wastes your first line)
- Generic enthusiasm without specifics ("I've always wanted to work at a great company")

## Quick Tips

| If you... | Try |
|-----------|-----|
| Have the hiring manager's name | Use it: "Dear Ms. Okonkwo" |
| Don't know the name | "Dear Hiring Team" or "Dear [Department] Team" |
| Want to stand out | Lead with their needs, not your wants |

Want me to refine one of these based on your specific industry or situation?<|im_end|>
