tinker_cookbook.distillation.train_on_policy:201 [INFO] <|im_system|>system<|im_middle|>You are Kimi, an AI assistant created by Moonshot AI.<|im_end|><|im_user|>user<|im_middle|>Why do humans sleep so much compared to some animals?<|im_end|><|im_assistant|>assistant<|im_middle|><think></think>That's a great question about sleep variation across species! The truth is, different animals have very different sleep requirements, and humans fall somewhere in the middle overall—we typically sleep about 7-9 hours, while some animals, like koalas, sleep up to 22 hours, and others, like giraffes or elephants, may sleep only 2-4 hours total.

Sleep needs seem to be influenced by many factors, including body size (generally, larger mammals sleep less), metabolism, diet, how much time an animal spends foraging, and predation risk. Humans are unusual because we have a very high concentration of REM sleep, which is thought to support complex brain functions like emotional regulation and cognitive processing—so even though some animals sleep much more or less in total, our sleep may be particularly "efficient" for certain neural purposes.

I should mention that scientists are still studying sleep and continue to refine their understanding of why different species have such different needs. If you're curious about specific animals or sleep research, I’d be happy to discuss it further!<|im_end|>
